## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design multi-tenant throughput controls with backpressure and strong delivery guarantees in KCNA.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation
- Token bucket rate limiting
- Backpressure signaling to producers
- Idempotent dedupe for at-least-once
- Quota-based configurability and Canary testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for per-tenant token bucket limiter
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst;
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens -= 1;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test under burst traffic across tenants and ensure fairness?
- How would you evolve this to multi-broker coordination with dynamic quota changes?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes hands-on experience building streaming joins with per-key state, TTL-based invalidation, and robust delivery guarantees in KCNA-like pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- Per-sensor TTL cache
- Event-time join with watermarks
- Side-input-driven re-enrichment
- Offset tracking and at-least-once semantics

## Code Example

```java
// Pseudo KCNA sink skeleton
class CalibratedJoinSink {
  Map<String, Calibration> cache;
  void onCalibration(Calibration c){ cache.put(c.sensorId, c); }
  void onReading(Reading r){
    Calibration c = cache.get(r.sensorId);
    r.factor = (c != null) ? c.factor : 1.0;
    emit(r);
    // optionally track buffered items for late calibration
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to handle calibration TTL expiry without missed re-enrichment?
- How to test robustness under high-cardinality sensors and bursty data?

