## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of differential privacy in a high-throughput, multi-tenant streaming system with per-tenant budgets and latency requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Differential privacy in streaming
- Per-tenant budget accounting and rollover
- Sliding window aggregations and latency SLAs
- Noise calibration (Gaussian) for min/max/median
- Data model and ledger design

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: budget check and noise application
function processBatch(batch, tenantBudget) {
  const window = computeWeeklyWindow(batch, tenantBudget.tenantId);
  const stats = computeMinMaxMedian(window.values);
  const noisy = addGaussianNoise(stats, tenantBudget.epsilon, tenantBudget.delta);
  emit(tenantBudget.tenantId, noisy);
  tenantBudget.decrease(epsilonFrom(noisy));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adapt epsilon dynamically per tenant based on data drift?
- How would you test budget exhaustion and ensure no data leakage during failovers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design robust, scalable schema evolution in a multi-tenant streaming system, including compatibility rules, rollouts, and testing under mixed versions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant schema registry
- Versioned event envelopes
- Backward/forward compatibility
- Rolling upgrades and dual writes
- Data migration and validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: coerce event to current schema version per tenant
function coerce(event, currentVersion) {
  if (event.schemaVersion === currentVersion) return event;
  if (currentVersion === 2 && event.schemaVersion === 1) {
    return { ...event, region: event.region ?? "unknown", schemaVersion: 2 };
  }
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle removing a field or changing types across tenants?
- How would you instrument metrics to detect schema drift and upgrade failures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner KCNA candidate's ability to implement a time-window join across topics, with late data, offset handling, and at-least-once guarantees. It requires simple in-memory state management, per-key routing, and clear API boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- Time-window joins across topics
- Late-arriving data handling
- Per-message offset tracking
- Idempotent emissions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch for in-memory join
class JoinEnricher {
  constructor() {
    this.latestAnnotation = new Map(); // serviceId -> { annotation, ts }
    this.metricBuffer = new Map(); // serviceId -> [{metric, ts}]
  }
  processMetric(metric, offset, ts) {
    const ann = this.latestAnnotation.get(metric.serviceId);
    const enriched = ann ? { ...metric, annotation: ann.annotation } : { ...metric, annotation: null };
    this.emitEnriched(enriched, offset);
  }
  processAnnotation(ann, offset, ts) {
    this.latestAnnotation.set(ann.serviceId, { annotation: ann.value, ts });
    // re-emit any buffered metrics for this service
    const buffered = this.metricBuffer.get(ann.serviceId) || [];
    for (const m of buffered) {
      this.emitEnriched({ ...m.metric, annotation: ann.value }, m.offset);
    }
    this.metricBuffer.set(ann.serviceId, []);
  }
  emitEnriched(record, offset) {
    // send to metrics_enriched and commit offset afterward
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses hands-on windowed aggregation, per-key state management, late-data handling, and correct offset commits in a beginner KCNA setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tumbling windows, per-key state, late data handling, TTL, idempotent emits, offset management.

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedAggSink {
  constructor(windowMs, latenessMs) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.latenessMs = latenessMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // windowStart -> deviceId -> sum
  }
  onMessage(msg) {
    // msg: { deviceId, value, timestamp, offset }
    const ws = this.windowStartFor(msg.timestamp);
    const bucket = (this.state.get(ws) = this.state.get(ws) || new Map());
    bucket.set(msg.deviceId, (bucket.get(msg.deviceId) || 0) + msg.value);
  }
  onWindowBoundary(now) {
    // emit and clean up windows that ended before now - latenessMx
  }
  commitOffsets() {
    // persist offsets after successful emits
  }
  getTotals() {
    // return current in-memory totals for inspection
  }
  windowStartFor(ts) {
    return Math.floor(ts / this.windowMs) * this.windowMs;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of access control in KCNA, multi-tenant isolation, and auditable, low-friction token rotation.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped ACLs and per-topic allowlists
- Token-based authentication with runtime rotation
- Non-blocking authorization checks and audit logs
- Backward-compatible ACL evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
// ACL data model and quick middleware example
const acl = {
  "tenantA": { topics: ["t1","t2"], actions: ["publish","subscribe"], version: 1 }
}
function authorize(tenant, action, topic, tokenVersion) {
  const entry = acl[tenant];
  if (!entry) return false;
  return entry.version === tokenVersion && entry.topics.includes(topic) && entry.actions.includes(action);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you store ACLs to support auditability and rotation without downtime?
- How would you test for token revocation affecting in-flight requests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical deployment pattern that minimizes blast radius when migrating schemas, emphasizing per-tenant isolation and real-world rollback scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Shadow topics per tenant
- Exactly-once semantics and deduping
- Offset alignment across duplicated streams
- Canary rollout and rollback mechanics
- Observability and test planning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-router: duplicates to prod and shadow per tenant
function routeMessage(msg, tenant) {
  const id = msg.id;
  publish(`prod.${tenant}`, { ...msg, shadow: false }, id);
  publish(`shadow.${tenant}`, { ...msg, shadow: true }, id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle backpressure if shadow topic lags?
- How to ensure protection against schema drift in shadow while keeping prod stable?

Why This Is Asked
Tests practical error handling and DLQ routing in KCNA pipelines, a common real-world requirement. Emphasizes per-topic isolation, backpressure, and safe offset management.

Key Concepts
- Dead-letter routing per topic
- Retry policy with backoff and max attempts
- Offset commits tied to successful processing
- Canary validation of DLQ behavior and downstream progress

Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(msg) {
  try {
    const payload = parse(msg.value);
    if (!isValid(payload)) throw new Error('invalid payload');
    process(payload);
    commitOffsets(msg); // on success
  } catch (e) {
    routeToDL(msg, e.message); // to sensor_DL or alerts_DL based on topic
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor DLQ growth and set alert thresholds?
- What strategies prevent DLQ messages from growing unbounded while ensuring no data loss in the main path?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design a scalable, auditable governance layer that operates within strict per-tenant boundaries while maintaining low latency in KCNA's streaming pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- Policy representation: graph-based rules, versioned, tenant-scoped
- Real-time evaluation: per-record decisions, caching, cache invalidation
- Privacy vs. usefulness: field-level masking vs. full redaction
- Auditability: tamper-evident logs, provenance tagging, verifiable proofs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluatePolicy(tenantId, topic, record) -> { maskFields: [..], sinks: [..], tags: [..] }
function evaluatePolicy(tenantId, topic, record) {
  const policy = policyCache.get(tenantId, topic);
  // apply masking rules
  for (const f of policy.maskFields) {
    if (record.hasOwnProperty(f)) record[f] = mask(record[f]);
  }
  // attach provenance tags
  record._tags = policy.tags;
  return { masked: record, sinks: policy.sinks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy drift during upgrades without downtime?
- How do you prove policy adherence during audits across backups and restores?
